// Practice quiz: Service-to-service calls: tokens, timeouts and failed downstream calls
// Unreviewed first draft, 2026-11-04. Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: containers-cicd/microservices-and-ci-cd/service-to-service-calls

::o1 header::Which header carries a bearer token on an HTTP call? {
~Token\: followed by the token#Not quite. That is not the standard field.
=Authorization\: Bearer followed by the token#Correct. RFC 6750 defines that header form.
~Cookie\: Bearer followed by the token#Not quite. Cookies are a different mechanism.
~A query parameter is always preferred#Not quite. Query strings end up in logs, so the header is preferred.
}

::o1 source::Which is the right place for a service token used by a nightly background job? {
~A constant in the source file#Not quite. Source files get shared and committed.
~A log line it writes at startup#Not quite. Tokens must never be logged.
=Configuration, such as an environment variable#Correct. No user request exists to forward, so the token comes from configuration.
~The image name#Not quite. Image names are public to anyone who can list them.
}

::o2 httpx default::You create httpx.Client() and set nothing. What happens if a server stops sending data? {
~It waits without limit#Not quite. That is what timeout\=None does.
~It retries forever#Not quite. HTTPX does not retry forever by default.
~It returns an empty 200#Not quite. A timeout is raised as an error.
=It raises a timeout error after about five seconds of inactivity#Correct. HTTPX applies a default limit.
}

::o2 connect vs read::A service accepts the connection and then sends nothing. Which limit ends this wait? {
~The connect timeout#Not quite. Connecting already finished.
~The pool timeout#Not quite. That limits waiting for a free connection from the pool.
~The DNS cache#Not quite. That has nothing to do with waiting for data.
=The read timeout#Correct. Connecting already succeeded, so the wait is for data.
}

::o2 restclient::Where are RestClient connect and read timeouts configured? {
=On the underlying client request factory#Correct. RestClient delegates to the request factory it was built with.
~In a retrieve() argument on each call#Not quite. There is no such timeout argument.
~In the Dockerfile#Not quite. A Dockerfile cannot set this.
~In the Authorization header#Not quite. Headers do not control the client.
}

::o3 status::Your read timeout on a downstream call expired. Which response is the clearest? {
~200 with an empty body#Not quite. That hides the failure.
=504 Gateway Timeout#Correct. No timely response arrived from upstream.
~404 Not Found#Not quite. The resource was not shown to be missing.
~401 Unauthorized#Not quite. The caller's credentials are not the problem.
}

::o3 garbage::A downstream service answers with HTML where your code expects JSON. Which response fits? {
=502 Bad Gateway#Correct. An invalid response arrived from the upstream service.
~504 Gateway Timeout#Not quite. A response did arrive.
~201 Created#Not quite. Nothing was created.
~An unhandled exception is fine#Not quite. That gives the caller an unclear error.
}
